module gunbc.test.claim.github_deployment_protection_rule_witness

// Witness file: the custom deployment protection rule upstream model is load-bearing or it is wrong.
// The feature this model exists for replaces "the operator clicks Review deployments" with "the
// operator taps a notification", which means it sits on the path that decides whether srv1 root
// mutation runs — and, unlike most of extdeps, it is a path where a SILENT failure is possible in both
// directions: a deployment that never runs, and a deployment that runs when it should not have.
//
// So the tests below are not "does the fold return true when everything is fine". Each one is written
// so that the WRONG model of the upstream returns the wrong answer, and the mutations are concentrated
// where a wrong answer would not be caught by anything else:
//   - the review state and the body's required fields, because an invented state or a body without an
//     environment is a request that releases a deployment nobody approved;
//   - the two review endpoints and their two success codes, because reading a poll's 200 as an
//     accepted review reports an approval that never happened;
//   - what the poll can and cannot say, because its reviewer list is User | Team and CANNOT identify
//     an App, so an implementation that looked for one there would be permanently closed;
//   - the timeout's DIRECTION, because a timeout that reads as approval is the failure mode of a gate.
// The decisions that are ours — which credential we permit, which axes we refuse to grant, what the
// broker checks against a capability, what must be witnessed before a human reviewer goes away — are
// modelled and witnessed in gunbc.auth.deployment_gate_authorization, not here. An upstream model that
// held them would be edited every time our policy changed.

